@startuml File System Tree Structure
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam objectBackgroundColor #ffffff
skinparam objectBorderColor #2c3e50
skinparam packageBackgroundColor #e8f4fd
skinparam packageBorderColor #3498db

title **File System Tree Structure - Composite Pattern Example**

package "File System Hierarchy" {
    object "My Drive" as root {
        name = "My Drive"
        type = Folder
        total_size = 1850 bytes
    }
    
    object "Documents" as docs {
        name = "Documents"
        type = Folder
        total_size = 150 bytes
    }
    
    object "Pictures" as pics {
        name = "Pictures"
        type = Folder
        total_size = 1200 bytes
    }
    
    object "document.txt" as file1 {
        name = "document.txt"
        type = File
        size = 150 bytes
    }
    
    object "image.jpg" as file2 {
        name = "image.jpg"
        type = File
        size = 1200 bytes
    }
    
    object "presentation.pptx" as file3 {
        name = "presentation.pptx"
        type = File
        size = 500 bytes
    }
}

' Tree relationships
root ||--o{ docs : contains
root ||--o{ pics : contains
root ||--o{ file3 : contains

docs ||--o{ file1 : contains
pics ||--o{ file2 : contains

note top of root : **Root Composite**\nContains other composites and leaves\nDelegates operations to children

note right of docs : **Composite Node**\nCan contain files and other folders\nAggregates sizes of children

note right of file1 : **Leaf Node**\nEnd point of tree structure\nNo children, contains actual data

note bottom of root
**Operation Examples:**
• root.get_size() → 1850 bytes (sum of all children)
• root.display() → Shows entire tree with indentation
• docs.get_size() → 150 bytes (sum of Documents folder)
• file1.get_size() → 150 bytes (individual file size)

**Composite Pattern Benefits:**
• Uniform treatment of files and folders
• Recursive operations work naturally
• Easy to add new file types or folder types
• Client code doesn't distinguish between leaves and composites
end note

@enduml
